<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
      Note for anyone editing the comments here: the rest of this repository
      punctuates an aside with a doubled ASCII hyphen, and XML forbids exactly
      that sequence inside a comment. The manifest merger then rejects the whole
      file with "Error parsing AndroidManifest.xml" and says nothing about which
      line or why. The em dashes below are therefore load-bearing rather than a
      style choice, and the encoding declaration above exists to make them
      explicit.
    -->
    <!-- The Flutter template only declares INTERNET in the debug and profile
         manifests (the tool needs it for hot reload), which is a classic trap: the
         app talks to the server on every screen, so a release APK built without
         this line installs fine and then fails every request with an opaque socket
         error. Declared here so it applies to all build types. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!--
      ===== Local reminders (F1) =====

      Everything below exists because of one product fact: a reminder that does
      not arrive makes this app pointless (see flutter-migration-plan.md, the
      section on why the PWA was abandoned). The previous PWA failed precisely
      here, so each of the lines below is a place where the feature silently
      stops working if it is absent.

      POST_NOTIFICATIONS is declared by the plugin itself since its v16, but it
      is repeated here on purpose: it is the permission the app has to *ask* for
      at runtime on Android 13+, and a reader of this manifest should see that
      the app posts notifications without having to go read a dependency's
      manifest. When it is denied, nothing throws — zonedSchedule succeeds, the
      alarm fires, and the system drops the notification. That silence is why the
      bench screen displays the flag.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>

    <!--
      Without this, Android forgets every scheduled alarm the moment the phone
      reboots, and there is no error anywhere: the queue is simply empty
      afterwards. Together with the ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver below, the
      plugin re-arms everything it had stored. "Survives a reboot" is an explicit
      acceptance criterion of F1 — a phone that reboots overnight for a system
      update must still produce the 09:00 reminder.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <!--
      Exact alarms, Android 12+.

      A day-granularity reminder does not need minute precision in principle,
      but an *inexact* alarm is batched with other wakeups and can drift by
      hours. "Напомнить утром" arriving at 14:00 is a different, much less useful
      product, so we start from exact and degrade only if the permission is
      missing (LocalNotificationGateway picks inexactAllowWhileIdle then, and the
      bench says so out loud).

      Both permissions are declared, and that is not redundancy:

      - SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is auto-granted on Android 12 but is user-revocable
        from Android 13 on, so the app must be able to ask for it
        (requestExactAlarmsPermission opens the system "Alarms & reminders" page);
      - USE_EXACT_ALARM is granted at install time and cannot be revoked, which
        is what makes the overnight test meaningful — but Google restricts it to
        apps whose core function is alarms/calendars and audits it on Play.
        TaskRadar is sideloaded onto one personal phone and will never be
        published (see flutter-migration-plan.md, F5), so the restriction does
        not apply. If that ever changes, drop this line and keep the one above.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM"/>

    <application
        android:label="TaskRadar"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
          The two receivers from flutter_local_notifications. Since the plugin's
          v16 they are NOT declared by the plugin's own manifest, so an app that
          forgets them compiles, installs, schedules alarms that appear in
          `pendingNotificationRequests()` — and shows nothing when they fire.
          There is no warning of any kind.

          ScheduledNotificationReceiver is what actually posts a scheduled
          notification when its alarm goes off.

          ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver re-arms everything after the events
          that wipe the alarm queue. BOOT_COMPLETED is the obvious one;
          MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED matters just as much during development, where the
          app is reinstalled several times a day and would otherwise silently
          lose the very alarm being tested. The two QUICKBOOT actions are the
          non-standard equivalents some HTC/Xiaomi ROMs send instead of
          BOOT_COMPLETED — harmless everywhere else, and this is exactly the
          class of device whose power management F1 is measuring.

          exported="false": they are only ever triggered by the system and by
          this app, so nothing else should be able to poke them.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
